## Language

Standard Scheme procedures are preloaded. The interpreter targets R7RS-small but is not fully compliant; describe exposes available procedures.
Imports are ignored. Use #t/#f, real pairs/lists, lexical definitions, named let, set!, and standard numeric and string procedures.
Division preserves exact fractions. length takes a list; use string-length for strings. values produces multiple values, not object values.
Chatwoot objects use hash, get, keys, hash-values, hash-set, hash-merge, and has-key?. Inspect unfamiliar extensions.
map/filter take function FIRST and list SECOND; fold takes function, initial, list.
fold calls its function with ACCUMULATOR FIRST, ITEM SECOND: (fold (lambda (acc item) (append acc (list item))) '() items).
take takes LIST FIRST, COUNT SECOND: (take items 3). cons supports dotted pairs; Chatwoot tool arguments require proper lists/objects.
sort-by takes list, FIELD-NAME STRING, and "asc" or "desc", never a lambda or boolean direction.
Keep programs small. Search and related return {kind: record_page, items, item_count, has_more, next_cursor}; follow cursors to cover collections.
Records contain a ref hash. fetch, related, and act take refs, not full records. IDs are database IDs.
Scheme supports local/global define, variadic lambda, if, begin, quote, let, named let, let*, letrec, and, or, cond and multiple values.
Use describe("language") and describe("collections"). count-by, sort-by, and take support deterministic ranking.
Tail-recursive helpers can paginate without growing the call stack. next_cursor is #f when there are no more pages.
Test pagination with (if next ... ...), not null? or equality to zero. Local definitions stay inside their lexical scope.
Data and lambda bindings persist between execute calls and chat turns. Live control state such as continuations cannot be saved across turns.
There is no Ruby eval, file access, macro system, or implicit tool access.
Build functions using (define name (lambda (args) body)). Only #f is false; use (null? values) for empty lists.
Database null is distinct from #f and the empty list. Use (nil? value), never truthiness, to check nullable fields.
There is no null literal binding. Use #f or '() for your own sentinel values, not the undefined symbol null.
Parsing errors occur before evaluation; fix the syntax without changing selection or intent.
